 ## Overview
-NeRF-Det Training Objectives define the loss functions used to jointly optimize a Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) branch and a 3D object detection branch in a single end-to-end framework. The method solves the problem of ambiguous volumetric representations in multi-view 3D object detection by leveraging the NeRF branch to learn a geometry-aware opacity field, which provides superior scene geometry modeling compared to explicit depth estimation or cost volumes [S1]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [KNN-NeRF](8672-knn-nerf.md) or [Progressive and Dynamic Scene Growth](8869-progressive-and-dynamic-scene-growth.md) by explicitly coupling the photometric/depth supervision of the radiance field with the detection head's classification and localization losses, ensuring that better NeRF optimization directly improves detection results [S1].
+NeRF-Det Training Objectives define the loss functions used to jointly optimize a Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) branch and a 3D object detection branch in a single end-to-end framework [S1]. The method solves the problem of ambiguous volumetric representations in multi-view 3D object detection by leveraging the NeRF branch to learn a geometry-aware opacity field, which provides superior scene geometry modeling compared to explicit depth estimation or cost volumes [S1]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [KNN-NeRF](8672-knn-nerf.md) or [Progressive and Dynamic Scene Growth](8869-progressive-and-dynamic-scene-growth.md) by explicitly coupling the photometric/depth supervision of the radiance field with the detection head's classification and localization losses, ensuring that better NeRF optimization directly improves detection results [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method jointly trains the detection and NeRF branches end-to-end. No per-scene optimization is performed for the NeRF branch at test time; the network is generalizable to unseen scenes [S1].
